# Line endings. One rule, deliberately.
#
# WHAT THIS PREVENTS. Without it git stores whatever bytes it is given, so any
# tool that rewrites a file on Windows can flip LF to CRLF and git then reports
# EVERY line as changed. Session 6 produced a 1,430-line diff for a four-line
# deletion exactly that way, and it has cost time twice since. The real damage
# is not the bytes: a pull request whose diff is the whole file does not get
# reviewed.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text and normalise it to LF in the
# repository. `eol=lf` then checks LF back out, so every working tree on every
# machine holds the same bytes as the repository.
#
# THE ALTERNATIVE, if LF in the working tree ever becomes a nuisance on Windows:
# drop `eol=lf` and keep `text=auto` alone. The repository still stores LF and
# the absurd diffs still go away; only the checkout becomes CRLF. It was not
# chosen because identical bytes everywhere is the simpler thing to reason
# about, and this project has been bitten by endings three times.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# NOTHING IS PINNED TO CRLF, and that is checked rather than assumed. Batch
# files are the usual exception --- cmd.exe needs CRLF --- and the repository
# contains no .bat, .cmd, .ps1 or .sh at all. Nor any binary: every one of the
# 288 tracked files is text. Adding a rule for files that do not exist is the
# kind of speculative decoration this project deletes, so when the first script
# arrives, pin it then:
#
#   *.bat           text eol=crlf
#   *.cmd           text eol=crlf
